Q: Nested List Printed in Columns A: <p>I have implemented a small method (and its “helpers”) to achieve what you want.</p>
<pre><code>countW = [[127, 0], [0, 92], [7, 8]]

def get_num_representation(num):
    # we add as many spaces as are missing from the
    # length of the number received to reach “3”,
    # this allows alignment to be maintained with
    # numbers up to three digits long.
    return &quot; &quot; * (3 - len(str(num))) + str(num)

def get_header(index, value):
    return f&quot;Value: {index}, Count: {get_num_representation(value)}    &quot;

def my_print(data_list):
    titles_separator = &quot;                &quot;
    rows = [&quot;     &quot;, &quot;&quot;, &quot;&quot;]
    for index, data in enumerate(data_list):
        rows[0] += f&quot;Answer {index} {titles_separator}&quot;
        rows[1] += get_header(0, data_list[index][0]) + &quot; &quot;
        rows[2] += get_header(1, data_list[index][1]) + &quot; &quot;

    for row in rows:
        print(row)

my_print(countW)
</code></pre>
<p>This prints:</p>
<pre><code>     Answer 0                 Answer 1                 Answer 2                 
Value: 0, Count: 127     Value: 0, Count:   0     Value: 0, Count:   7     
Value: 1, Count:   0     Value: 1, Count:  92     Value: 1, Count:   8     
</code></pre>
<p>If you look at the code, you will notice that the calls to <strong>get_header()</strong> (bad name) and <strong>get_num()</strong> could be avoided by using their code in the line that calls them, but I preferred to make it more complex, but more readable.</p>

Q: SELECT rows WHERE a DATETIME column has one of multiple qualifying DATE values using CodeIgniter A: <p>As a CodeIgniter model method, it makes sense to pass in an array of whitelisted dates and only cast the dateadd value as a DATE once.  The parentheses in the DATE expression will implicitly disable identifier quoting for the entire expression. This implements query logic where the value is one date OR another date OR ...etc.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getOrdersforDates(array $dates): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;where_in('DATE(dateadd)', $dates ?: null)
        -&gt;get('orders')
        -&gt;result();
}
</code></pre>
<p>If the whitelist array is empty, the above script will not render a WHERE clause.</p>
